 ## Background
-The concept of regularizing NeRF training emerged as researchers identified that minimizing photometric error alone was insufficient for robust reconstruction, particularly in sparse-view or ill-posed scenarios. Early works like RegNeRF [S1] introduced geometric smoothness and color priors to prevent overfitting. Subsequent developments incorporated generative priors, such as Diffusion-regularized NeRF [S1], to enforce realistic geometry and texture distributions. More recent approaches leverage semantic understanding via CLIP [S1, S3] or structural similarity metrics like S3IM [S1] to improve perceptual quality. The family also includes specialized losses for specific tasks, such as volumetric supervision for dynamic scenes [S1] and style/saturation losses for object insertion [S1, S6].
+The concept of regularizing NeRF training emerged as a solution to the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering, particularly in sparse-view settings where photometric loss alone leads to artifacts like "floaters" or blurry geometry [S1, 8582-regnerf.md, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md]. Early work like RegNeRF introduced geometric smoothness and color priors to stabilize training from few shots [S1, 8582-regnerf.md]. Subsequent developments incorporated generative priors from diffusion models [S1, 7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md], semantic consistency via CLIP [S1, S3, 11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md], and structural similarity metrics like S3IM [S1, 10894-multiplex-training-via-s3im.md, 10895-s3im-stochastic-structural-similarity.md] to improve perceptual quality. The field continues to expand with specialized losses for specific artifacts, such as occlusion elimination [S1], cache consistency in inverse rendering [S3], and confidence-based calibration for joint pose optimization [S7].
 
